WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.909
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book one.

2
00:00:05.209 --> 00:00:08.619
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.919 --> 00:00:16.180
Unit three: Research & Analysis. Today you will report research procedures objectively using the passive voice.

4
00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:19.144
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:23.089
Part one: words. You will learn ten new words.

6
00:00:23.950 --> 00:00:25.056
Methodology.

7
00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:30.163
Methodology means the system of methods and principles used in a study.

8
00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:33.818
The methodology was criticised for its small sample.

9
00:00:34.417 --> 00:00:37.510
Your turn. Say: Methodology.

10
00:00:40.367 --> 00:00:41.588
Methodology.

11
00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:43.734
Data analysis.

12
00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:48.851
Data analysis means the process of inspecting and interpreting data.

13
00:00:49.450 --> 00:00:52.692
Data analysis revealed an unexpected pattern.

14
00:00:53.283 --> 00:00:56.354
Your turn. Say: Data analysis.

15
00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:00.886
Data analysis.

16
00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:03.359
Hypothesis testing.

17
00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:09.066
Hypothesis testing means statistical evaluation of whether data support a prediction.

18
00:01:09.650 --> 00:01:13.136
Hypothesis testing indicated no significant difference.

19
00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:17.459
Your turn. Say: Hypothesis testing.

20
00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:21.998
Hypothesis testing.

21
00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:23.774
Variable.

22
00:01:24.783 --> 00:01:27.816
Variable means a factor that can change and be measured.

23
00:01:28.417 --> 00:01:30.939
Income was treated as a control variable.

24
00:01:31.550 --> 00:01:34.075
Your turn. Say: Variable.

25
00:01:36.933 --> 00:01:38.074
Variable.

26
00:01:38.817 --> 00:01:39.829
Correlation.

27
00:01:40.817 --> 00:01:44.918
Correlation means a statistical relationship between two variables.

28
00:01:45.517 --> 00:01:48.886
A weak correlation was observed between screen time and sleep.

29
00:01:49.483 --> 00:01:52.298
Your turn. Say: Correlation.

30
00:01:55.133 --> 00:01:56.216
Correlation.

31
00:01:56.950 --> 00:01:57.986
Peer review.

32
00:01:58.983 --> 00:02:02.887
Peer review means evaluation of research by independent experts.

33
00:02:03.483 --> 00:02:06.110
The study has not yet undergone peer review.

34
00:02:06.717 --> 00:02:09.694
Your turn. Say: Peer review.

35
00:02:12.533 --> 00:02:13.523
Peer review.

36
00:02:14.283 --> 00:02:15.145
Findings.

37
00:02:16.150 --> 00:02:19.450
Findings means the conclusions reached after investigation.

38
00:02:20.050 --> 00:02:22.735
The findings were replicated in two other countries.

39
00:02:23.350 --> 00:02:25.979
Your turn. Say: Findings.

40
00:02:28.833 --> 00:02:29.904
Findings.

41
00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:31.756
Objectivity.

42
00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:36.236
Objectivity means freedom from personal bias in judgement.

43
00:02:36.850 --> 00:02:39.685
Funding from industry may compromise objectivity.

44
00:02:40.283 --> 00:02:43.040
Your turn. Say: Objectivity.

45
00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:47.122
Objectivity.

46
00:02:47.883 --> 00:02:48.850
Sampling.

47
00:02:49.850 --> 00:02:53.870
Sampling means the selection of a subset to represent a population.

48
00:02:54.483 --> 00:02:57.238
Random sampling minimises selection bias.

49
00:02:57.850 --> 00:03:00.189
Your turn. Say: Sampling.

50
00:03:03.033 --> 00:03:04.011
Sampling.

51
00:03:04.750 --> 00:03:05.798
Validity.

52
00:03:06.783 --> 00:03:10.582
Validity means the extent to which a study measures what it claims to.

53
00:03:11.183 --> 00:03:14.530
The validity of self-reported data is often questioned.

54
00:03:15.117 --> 00:03:17.316
Your turn. Say: Validity.

55
00:03:20.167 --> 00:03:21.121
Validity.

56
00:03:21.933 --> 00:03:25.805
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

57
00:03:26.650 --> 00:03:31.134
Visal, could you walk me through how your study on sleep and exam results was carried out?

58
00:03:32.250 --> 00:03:39.361
Certainly. Participants were recruited from three schools, and random sampling was used to reduce bias.

59
00:03:40.450 --> 00:03:42.577
And how was the information processed?

60
00:03:43.683 --> 00:03:51.758
The data was analyzed. The hypothesis was tested rigorously using two different statistical models.

61
00:03:52.850 --> 00:03:55.279
Why not simply write 'we analysed the data'?

62
00:03:56.383 --> 00:04:04.132
Because in a methods section, what was done matters more than who did it. The passive keeps the focus objective.

63
00:04:05.267 --> 00:04:06.906
Part three: grammar.

64
00:04:07.750 --> 00:04:11.468
Grammar focus: Passive voice for methodology.

65
00:04:11.768 --> 00:04:13.024
Look at the pattern.

66
00:04:13.717 --> 00:04:17.553
The data was analyzed. The hypothesis was tested rigorously.

67
00:04:18.450 --> 00:04:21.599
Participants were recruited from three secondary schools.

68
00:04:22.483 --> 00:04:25.795
Two statistical models were applied to confirm the results.

69
00:04:26.733 --> 00:04:36.816
Methods sections use the past simple passive because the procedure, not the researcher, is the focus. Adverbs such as 'rigorously' or 'randomly' go before the past participle.

70
00:04:37.550 --> 00:04:41.607
Be careful! Don't say: The data were rigorously analyse.

71
00:04:43.567 --> 00:04:46.321
Say: The data were rigorously analysed.

72
00:04:46.621 --> 00:04:48.887
The passive needs the past participle.

73
00:04:49.700 --> 00:04:52.201
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

74
00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:54.489
How a Study Is Built.

75
00:04:54.789 --> 00:04:59.784
Every credible study rests on a transparent methodology that others can replicate.

76
00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:05.621
Participants are typically selected through random sampling, which minimises the risk of bias.

77
00:05:06.633 --> 00:05:13.056
Once the data was analyzed, the hypothesis was tested rigorously against alternative explanations.

78
00:05:14.067 --> 00:05:19.340
A correlation, however strong, is never treated as proof that one variable causes another.

79
00:05:20.333 --> 00:05:24.469
Only after peer review are the findings considered robust enough to be published.

80
00:05:25.567 --> 00:05:29.847
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

81
00:05:30.683 --> 00:05:33.600
The hypothesis blank rigorously using two models.

82
00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:37.832
A, tested. B, was tested. C, has testing.

83
00:05:41.367 --> 00:05:43.262
The answer is B: was tested.

84
00:05:44.417 --> 00:05:47.531
Which sentence is most appropriate for a methods section?

85
00:05:47.831 --> 00:05:55.977
A, We asked lots of students some questions.. B, Students were asked questions by us, okay?. C, Two hundred students were surveyed..

86
00:05:59.533 --> 00:06:02.764
The answer is C: Two hundred students were surveyed..

87
00:06:03.917 --> 00:06:06.206
blank minimises selection bias.

88
00:06:06.506 --> 00:06:10.880
A, Random sampling. B, Peer review. C, Objectivity.

89
00:06:14.433 --> 00:06:17.013
The answer is A: Random sampling.

90
00:06:18.150 --> 00:06:21.984
If a test does not measure what it claims to measure, it lacks blank.

91
00:06:22.284 --> 00:06:26.286
A, correlation. B, validity. C, sampling.

92
00:06:29.833 --> 00:06:32.344
The answer is B: validity.

93
00:06:33.483 --> 00:06:36.423
According to the reading, a strong correlation blank.

94
00:06:36.723 --> 00:06:43.488
A, proves causation. B, is never treated as proof of causation. C, replaces peer review.

95
00:06:47.033 --> 00:06:50.751
The answer is B: is never treated as proof of causation.

96
00:06:51.967 --> 00:06:55.060
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

97
00:06:55.917 --> 00:06:58.578
Why is peer review important in research?

98
00:07:15.383 --> 00:07:16.860
Now listen to Visal.

99
00:07:17.160 --> 00:07:48.242
Peer review is important because it acts as a quality filter before research reaches the public. Methods and data are scrutinised by independent experts, so errors and weak conclusions can be identified. For example, a flawed sample might be spotted and corrected before publication. Admittedly, the process is slow and reviewers are not always objective. Even so, it remains the best safeguard we have for scientific credibility.

100
00:07:48.542 --> 00:07:50.344
Excellent answer, Visal!

101
00:07:51.250 --> 00:07:56.294
Great work! Now you can use ten new words and passive voice for methodology.

102
00:07:56.594 --> 00:08:01.417
Today's value is critical thinking. Think carefully and give reasons for your opinions.

103
00:08:01.717 --> 00:08:04.242
See you in Unit four. Goodbye!

104
00:08:04.542 --> 00:08:07.717
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

